From: grigsby@agames.com (JUMP IN THE FIRE)
Date: Tue, 9 Aug 1994 20:29:11 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-8-101] PHIL: knowledge

Ed Regis said:

> This is a free country and you can believe that objective reality is not
> knowable if you want to, but to claim that its is not knowable *by
> definition* is something else again.  What definition do you have in mind?  

objective (adj.) - existing indcependent of mind: relating to an object
as it is in itself or as distinguished from the consciousness of the
subject

subjective (adj.) - characteristing of or belinging to reality as
perceived or known as opposed to reality as it is in itself or
independent of mind

> >being 100% certain is equal to faith,...
> 
> Faith is belief without evidence.  Certainty is not by itself equivalent to
> faith.  Is it really a matter of "faith" to be 100% certain that 1+1 =2, or
> that you're more than two minutes old?  It's far more likely that your
> epistemology is in error than that these things are not 100% certain.  

You could have popped into existence one minute ago with memories of 
everything else...your questioner could be counting in binary, or
a foreign language...not likely, but possible.

Certainty is possible, but it implies a future rejection of
input that doesn't fit the certainty.  The way most of us
got on this list was by _not_ rejecting some piece of Extropian
thought that we stumbled over.

We all live our lives based on assumptions of the future; I don't
pretend they are more than assumptions.  Faith is calling these
assumptions "truth" or "objective reality".

