Subject: [#94-8-106] Re Reply knowledge

grigsby@agames.com (Tue, Aug 9, 1994 10:27 PM EST) writes:

>objective (adj.) - existing indcependent of mind: relating to an object as
it is in itself or as distinguished from the consciousness of the subject

Fine.  How does it follow from this definition that knowledge of those
objects is impossible?  You may not think it's possible, but that conclusion
surely doesn't follow *by definition.*

>Faith is calling these assumptions "truth" or "objective reality". 

But there is more available to epistemology than assumptions, and you live
your life by more than mere assumptions.  There's stuff that's justified by
evidence and stuff that isn't; there are warranted assumptions and
unwarranted assumptions; there is subjective knowledge and there is objective
knowledge.  To reject objective knowledge because you imagine it makes
Extropianism more plausible is paying a pretty high price for what amounts to
a personal preference.  

>Certainty is possible...

Okay, how?  

BTW, 1+1 = 2 is true even in "foreign" languages.  And it could not be a
statement in binary, because binary does not admit of the numeral 2.  



