From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Date: Wed, 10 Aug 1994 10:12:23 -0400
Subject: [#94-8-107] Individualism vs. Groupism

   Same question can be applied to our cells: do they have to serve
the interests of the whole body, or should they be individualistic?
 
   OK, cells are dumb.  Then, what about different parts of my Society
of Mind - my own personality architecture?  There is a part of me that
wants to pig out, there is a part that wants to feel and look good,
there is a part that is making me type this letter right now, and
there is one telling me that I should go to work and concentrate on
my career - and all of them are pretty conscious of their wishes.
Must some of them get disciplined for the good of the Whole? (what
is a person, anyway - a chunk of meat with lots of conflicting
things living there?)
 
   Why do we view ideal societies as giving complete freedom to their
members, and ideal 'living beings' as commanding complete control over
their parts?   Is it just because of where we see ourselves?
 
   If you see a distributed information system, how do you tell whether
it is a person or a society?
 
   I would suggest that instead of arguing whether this or that
particular system is conscious, we try to concentrate on the general
aspect of this issue:
   Suppose you have a multi-level system, and each of the levels has
its own degree of intelligence.  How do you regulate their relations?
Do they respect/care about each other?   Do you let a social structure
or your own organ perish only because it's no longer useful?  What if they
do it to you?   (I'd note that hierarchical systems are a simple case).
 
   In practice, the inter-system-level relations develop depending on
relative intelligences and strengths.  A strong urge to pig out can
defeat all consideration of personal or social value, a strong society
wins over a weak individual and vice versa, etc.
 
  If we want to approach these issues intelligently, we better build
a systemic theory of ethics, rather than just passionately exclaiming
that all our parts should be completely controlled, while the systems
that we are parts of  should leave us completely free.
 
  For now, we may live with the concept that meat bodies are the only
ones that should be free, but after uploading, the system ethics questions
will stare right into our [collective? ;-) ]  face.
 
Sasha.

