From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Date: Wed, 10 Aug 94 13:38:49 EDT
Subject: [#94-8-108] Individualism vs. Groupism

Sasha asks a good question...

>    Why do we view ideal societies as giving complete freedom to their
> members, and ideal 'living beings' as commanding complete control over
> their parts?   Is it just because of where we see ourselves?

Libertarians are often prime examples of that paradox, counting on
almost impossible ideals of discipline in thought and action--at 
least beyond what looks like the point of diminishing returns to
disorganized me.  But standard libs were concentrating on how
society should respect the individual, rather than on how the
mind works internally (which we extrops are into).  Reason and
ethics were tools against an unreasonable, immoral situation.

I think in societies and "individuals" there can be cohesiveness and
even identity without necessarily coercion.  My favorite psychologist,
Fritz Perls, had a method intended to integrate people by giving 
their parts freedom (but getting them to talk to each other).
It's true at the social level, too, that coerced togetherness
actually reduces social cohesion compared to free association.

Also, at all levels of any organization there's a wisdom to 
respecting the boundaries of the next level down (...country, state, 
county, town, neighborhood, family, individual...).  So things
don't completely flip-flop when you get to the individual level.

At some level (or, in the relations between an organization and
a small number of parts that aren't much smaller than the whole) it 
makes sense to have a more tightly-knit setup than a market because the 
overhead of negotiating prices and accounting for all transactions
is too high for the payoff.  And at some fine grain, it stops
making sense to look at the parts as "free" because they're just 
wired into place.

I think Sasha's paradox arises because we're not used to systems 
thinking, we don't understand intelligence yet, and we haven't
bridged between conscious-planning knowledge and market-process
knowledge.

Also because the parts of our minds are presently stuck sharing
bodies.  The brain is like the proverbial crowded theater.

