From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Wed, 10 Aug 94 12:25:01 -0700
Subject: [#94-8-110] EPIST: ah yes, once more!

The following comments are addressed only to those of us who have
strong opinions about epistemology; I know that's not a majority of
the members of the mailing list. (And yes, I'll probably be publishing
another version of the FAQ sometime today.)

Now that we're dragging the ideas of certainty and objective knowledge
all over here again, can we just go ahead and put Bartley's THE
RETREAT TO COMMITMENT on the reading list? The long list, not the
short one. It's certainly no more difficult than Gauthier's MORALS BY
AGREEMENT or Churchland's A NEUROCOMPUTATIONAL PERSPECTIVE, and we
seem to get into disputes about epistemology more often than we
disagree about the usefulness of neurocomputational approaches to
cognitive science and more often (or at least, more heatedly) than we
disagree about foundations of ethics.

There's also Bartley and Radnitzky's anthology, EVOLUTIONARY
EPISTEMOLOGY, which is a little more up-to-date, and doesn't spend so
many pages on direct polemics against the continental Christian
Existentialists (Karl Barth et cetera).

I'm not suggesting that Bartley, Campbell, Radnitzky, Popper,
Waechterhaeuser, and so forth are necessarily more EC than anyone
else, but I *do* think that familiarity with their ideas might address
some of the perplexities and smooth some of the more virulent
disagreements.

